4. Adverse Event Description (MedDRA Coding)
Reported Event: Severe generalized erythematous maculopapular rash, facial edema, and pruritus starting on Day 3 of therapy.
MedDRA Lowest Level Term (LLT): Generalized maculopapular rash / Facial swelling
MedDRA Preferred Term (PT): Drug eruption, Angioedema
5. Seriousness & Severity Classification
Severity: Severe (interfered significantly with normal daily function)
Seriousness Criteria: Serious (Medically Significant — required urgent outpatient intervention and systemic corticosteroid administration to avoid progression/hospitalization)
6. Dechallenge and Rechallenge
Dechallenge: Positive. Symptoms improved significantly within 48 hours following discontinuation of Amoxicillin/Clavulanate and administration of Cetirizine and Dexamethasone.
Rechallenge: Not performed (contraindicated due to risk of anaphylaxis).
7. Causality Assessment (WHO-UMC & Naranjo Scale)
Naranjo Algorithm Score: 6 (Probable)
Established reaction history (+1)
Event occurred after suspected drug administered (+2)
Adverse event improved when drug was discontinued (+1)
No alternative causes identified that solely explain the acute reaction (+2)
WHO-UMC Causality Category: Probable / Likely (plausible time relationship, positive dechallenge, unlikely attributable to concurrent disease or other drugs).
8. Drug Safety Narrative / Case Summary
A 54-year-old male with a history of hypertension and diabetes developed a severe maculopapular rash accompanied by facial swelling on Day 3 of a course of Amoxicillin/Clavulanic Acid prescribed for acute bacterial sinusitis. The suspect drug was immediately withdrawn. Treatment with an oral antihistamine and systemic corticosteroid resulted in resolution of symptoms over the following two days. Based on chronological compatibility, known drug class safety profile, and positive dechallenge, the adverse event is classified as serious (medically significant) and probably drug-related.